{"op":"write","path":"/tasklet/agent/home/jeevidya/JEEVidya_Definitive_Plan_v3.md","content":"# JEEVidya Pipeline — The Definitive Plan (v3)\n\n**Constraints accepted:** 100% free/libre tooling, local-first, no time ceiling, no complexity ceiling. Target horizon: 12–18 months. This is the terminal plan — v4 should never need to exist, because every decision below is either (a) correct by construction, (b) gated on rendered evidence, or (c) behind a contract that makes the decision swappable later without rework.\n\n---\n\n## Part I — Doctrine (what \"definitive\" means, operationally)\n\nEvery item in this plan obeys five laws. When in doubt during execution, resolve the doubt by these laws, in this order:\n\n1. **Make bug classes unrepresentable, not bug instances fixed.** No hand-maintained lists, no duplicated constants, no \"remember to.\" If a mistake *can* recur, a mechanism (derivation, lint, CI test) must make it impossible.\n2. **Measure before opinion.** No fix is \"done\" on vibes. Every fix has a number, a diff strip, or a unit test that moves. The harness (Phase 0) is the constitution; everything else is legislation.\n3. **Free means free *and* sovereign.** Prefer local, offline, open-license tools over free-tier cloud services. A free tier is a paid tool with a delay. Cloud-free tools (edge-tts) are permitted only as *bridges* with a named local successor.\n4. **Determinism is a feature, not a nicety.** Same inputs → bit-identical output. Every source of nondeterminism (seeds, thread ordering, float accumulation, wall-clock) is found and pinned. Without this, the cache, the harness, and your own eyes are all lying to you.\n5. **Evidence-gated investment.** Expensive work (segmented limb art, learned models, retroflex visemes) happens only after a gate whose pass/fail criteria were written down *before* the evidence was gathered.\n\n---\n\n## Part II — Phase −1: Governance & Ground Rules (week 0, half a day)\n\nCheap, boring, and the reason this plan survives a year.\n\n- **ADRs (Architecture Decision Records).** A `docs/adr/` folder; one short markdown file per irreversible decision (timing source, viseme inventory, color pipeline, gate outcomes). Every gate in this plan produces an ADR with the evidence frames linked. This is what stops Gate 1 from being relitigated from memory in month 9 — v2 wanted this (\"document the decision\"); v3 makes it a standing mechanism.\n- **License ledger.** `docs/LICENSES.md`: every external tool/model/dataset, its license, and whether it permits your use (note especially: anything **CC-BY-NC** is banned if JEEVidya ever monetizes — this rules out some Meta MMS checkpoints, see Gate 2). Reviewed whenever a new dependency lands. This is the \"everything free\" constraint made enforceable rather than aspirational.\n- **Artifact versioning.** Golden baselines, reference audio, and model weights are content-addressed and tracked with **DVC** (Apache-2.0) or plain content-hash directories — never committed raw to git, never floating on disk.\n- **Toolchain pinning.** `uv`/lockfile for Python, pinned ffmpeg build, pinned model checkpoints by hash. \"It renders differently on the new laptop\" must be impossible.\n- **Code health floor:** `ruff` + `mypy --strict` on all new/touched code; pre-commit hooks. Free, and they pay for themselves by week 3.\n\n---\n\n## Part III — Phase 0: Recon & The Harness\n\n### 0.1 Recon — the four unread files (unchanged from v2, still first)\n\nRead `scriptwriter.py`, `cache.py`, `rig_builder.py`, `audio_forge.py`. Answer v2's four questions, plus two new ones:\n\n- **`audio_forge.py`:** in addition to the pre-reverb tap point — what is the exact sample rate / hop discipline of the chain? (Gate 2's frame-accuracy math depends on it.)\n- **`scriptwriter.py`/`prompts.py`:** in addition to emotion vocabulary — does the director emit any *timing* or *emphasis* hints today? If yes, Track F can consume them; if no, Track F adds them.\n\n**Exit criterion:** one-page notes file; adjust affected items; nothing else blocks.\n\n### 0.2 The Harness (still the single most valuable item — now industrial-grade)\n\nThe v2 harness was a good camera. The v3 harness is a full metrology lab. Versioned with the repo, runnable with one command, and it is the *only* arbiter of \"better.\"\n\n**Reference corpus — 12 turns, not 6.** v2's six failure modes, plus:\n- (g) rapid-fire short sentences (tests pause/breath modeling, C8)\n- (h) a long monotone derivation read (tests idle motion & blink realism — the boredom stress test)\n- (i) English-heavy code-switched line (\"iss equation mein velocity substitute karo\") — JEE Hindi is Hinglish; the taxonomy and G2P must survive it\n- (j) numbers, units, and symbols (\"9.8 m/s²\", \"sin²θ\") — the text-normalization gauntlet\n- (k) whispered/soft delivery (tests amplitude envelope floor)\n- (l) a full 60-second \"showcase\" turn — the north-star artifact (Part X)\n\n**Objective metrics (all free/open):**\n- **Frame diff:** per-frame SSIM + max-abs-diff vs. baseline, `--frames A-B` zoom (as v2).\n- **Perceptual diff:** **LPIPS** (BSD) for \"does this look different to a human,\" because SSIM is blind to exactly the ghosting artifacts Track B fights.\n- **Full-clip quality:** **VMAF** (BSD+patent grant, ships in ffmpeg via libvmaf) against baseline for Track D's \"globally sharper\" claims.\n- **Lip-sync, scored automatically:** **SyncNet / LSE-C & LSE-D** (the Wav2Lip evaluation protocol — open source; verify repo license in the ledger). This is the crown jewel of the v3 harness: lip-sync quality becomes *a number in CI*, not a squint. Every Track C item's acceptance includes \"LSE-C does not regress; target improves by ≥X.\" Calibrate X against 3 real human-teacher videos scored with the same tool — that's your ceiling reference.\n- **Audio metrics:** RMS envelope plots, silence-gap detection, and (for TTS migration in Gate 2) a small MOS-style self-rating sheet — 5-point scale, rated blind, filename-shuffled by the harness so you can't cheat.\n- **Spring/camera telemetry:** per-frame CSV of every dynamic system (camera spring, mouth envelope, pose blend_t) + auto-generated plots. Tuning happens against plots, never against squinting (v2's B5 idea, generalized to *every* dynamic).\n\n**Correctness machinery:**\n- **Determinism check:** render twice, assert bit-identical. Any noise source found is pinned (seeds) or documented in an ADR with justification. Runs in CI forever.\n- **Pure-function test suite** seeded with v2's two tests (debounce sequence; coarticulation boundary-alpha), grown by every track.\n- **Property-based testing** with **Hypothesis** (MPL-2.0): generate random Devanagari/Latin/mixed strings and assert invariants — `visemes_for_word` never crashes, never emits out-of-enum visemes, spans are contiguous and non-negative, schwa deletion never deletes a written matra. This finds the conjunct edge case your 30-word fixture doesn't contain.\n- **Mutation testing** (`mutmut`, free) on the pure speech/phonology modules once they stabilize — proves the test suite actually constrains behavior.\n- **CI:** GitHub Actions free tier for lint/unit/property tests; a **self-hosted runner** on your own machine (free) for render-based harness jobs that need the asset tree and horsepower.\n\n**Exit criterion for Phase 0:** baselines committed for all 12 turns *before any fix lands*; determinism green; SyncNet scoring runs end-to-end on turn (c); one-command harness invocation documented.\n\n---\n\n## Part IV — Canonical Data Contracts (define once; everything targets these)\n\nAll of v2's contracts survive; two are extended, three are new. Contracts are frozen dataclasses with schema-version fields, round-trip serialization tests, and Hypothesis-generated fuzz coverage.\n\n### C-1. `PhonemeTiming` / `VisemeSpan` (extended)\n```\nPhonemeEvent(phoneme, t_start, t_end, word_id, confidence, source)\nVisemeSpan(viseme, t_start, t_end, source)\nVisemeTrack.sample(t) -> (viseme_from, viseme_to, blend_t)\n# source ∈ {\"uniform\", \"tts_native\", \"mfa\", \"ctc_align\", \"manual\"}\n```\n- The taxonomy in `visemes.py` (upgraded per C1 below) is the *only* viseme enum in the system.\n- Adapter v0 wraps today's uniform splits (`confidence=None`). Downstream code never knows which adapter is live. (v2's key unblocking move — unchanged.)\n- New: `manual` source + a tiny fixture format, so hand-marked ground-truth timings for the reference clips live in the same contract the pipeline consumes. Gate 2's evaluation then compares candidate sources against `manual` using identical code paths.\n\n### C-2. `PoseSample` → `JointState` (unchanged from v2)\nTuple form `(pose_from, pose_to, blend_t)` threaded through `PuppetActor → BoneEngine.render()` now; `JointState({joint: angle})` is the drop-in upgrade iff Gate 1 passes.\n\n### C-3. Constants module + duplication lint (unchanged from v2)\nOne `constants.py`/config dataclass; CI grep-lint fails on any duplicate definition of a listed name. Kills the `INTER_TURN_PADDING_MS` bug class permanently.\n\n### C-4. `ProsodyTrack` (new)\n```\nProsodyTrack: f0(t), energy(t), voicing(t), emphasis_spans[]\n```\nExtracted from the synthesized audio with **Praat via parselmouth** (GPL — fine for internal tooling) or **CREPE** (MIT) for F0, plus RMS energy. One extraction, cached, consumed by: amplitude envelope (C4), prosody-driven head motion (B8), gesture triggering (B9), and emphasis-driven camera nudges (B5+). Today four future features would each re-derive audio features independently; this contract makes prosody a first-class, computed-once track.\n\n### C-5. `GestureScript` / `CameraScript` (new)\nDeclarative, time-stamped event lists (gesture id, target, intensity; shot preset, cut time, hold) produced by direction logic (Track F) and consumed by motion/camera systems. Today these decisions are smeared through rendering code; extracting them makes the director testable (golden-file diffs on the *script*, cheap) separately from the renderer (expensive).\n\n### C-6. `SpriteManifest` (new)\nMachine-readable manifest per character: every sprite, its taxonomy slot (viseme class, emotion, limb segment), pivot annotations, alpha discipline, authored-at resolution. The C1/E1 handoff contract — new art drops in with **zero code changes**, and a CI check fails if the manifest promises a slot the taxonomy defines but the art lacks (or vice versa). This is v2's \"naming/format spec\" upgraded into a validated artifact.\n\n---\n\n## Part V — Track A: Build System & Cache Correctness\n\n*(v2's Track A, hardened. Short, gating, immediately after Phase 0.)*\n\n**A1. Content-addressed derivation cache (the full fix).**\nv2's import-graph fingerprint was the right direction; go all the way to the Bazel/Nix mental model:\n- A render stage's cache key = hash of (**code closure**: import-graph walk from the stage's entry point over first-party modules, file-content hashed) × (**input artifact hashes**) × (**parameters**) × (**tool versions**: ffmpeg build, model checkpoint hashes).\n- Asset files (sprites, fonts, LUTs) enter via the `SpriteManifest` and data manifests — hashed as inputs, never \"trusted by path.\"\n- **CI completeness test:** run a full render under an import tracer; assert every first-party module actually imported appears in some stage's closure. This is the fallback v2 defined, kept *in addition to* derivation — belt and suspenders, because the cache is the epistemology of the whole project.\n- If 0.1 reveals `cache.py` fights this design, refactor `cache.py`. The year-long horizon makes \"awkward, work around it\" the wrong call — this is the one place v3 chooses refactor over accommodation.\n\n**A2. Dead code:** confirm `render_stream()` unused (grep + runtime import assert over one full render), delete. Duplicate constants die via C-3.\n\n**A3. Cache-bust discipline:** every output-touching PR shows a cache-miss in the harness log (unchanged from v2 — cheap, permanent).\n\n**A4 (new). Render observability:** structured per-stage logs (stage, key, hit/miss, wall time, peak RSS) + a one-page HTML render report per harness run. When something is slow or stale in month 8, you'll know in one glance which stage and why.\n\n**Done when:** edit-any-first-party-file → cache miss; completeness test green; determinism green; constants lint green.\n\n---\n\n## Part VI — Track B: Motion\n\n*(v2's B1–B5 preserved verbatim in intent; B6–B9 are new and are what separates \"fixed\" from \"alive.\")*\n\n**B1. Pose blend, done properly** — exactly as v2: thread C-2, bend+blend both torso candidates, cache each `(pose, bend_params)` render independently (one extra composite, not 2× render; quantize bend params), directional breakdown blur via `whip_blur` along the dominant displacement vector peaking at `blend_t≈0.5`. Acceptance: turn (a) frame-difference energy is a smooth hump; no double edges at 100% zoom; LPIPS spike at the switch drops below threshold set from baseline.\n\n**B2. Debounce fix** — candidate-vs-committed state; the Phase-0 unit test; `TODO(post-C3)` re-review for double-smoothing (all as v2).\n\n**B3. GATE 1 — segmented arms** — as v2, with its written pass/fail criteria (≥2 frequently-used gestures still ghost at normal speed after B1, *and* `rig_builder.py` supports per-limb layers without rewrite). Outcome recorded as an ADR with evidence frames. If pass → E3 art + quadratic-profile FK extended to shoulder/elbow + `JointState` upgrade. If fail → B1 is the shipped answer, forever documented.\n\n**B4. Head-nod & blink** — swap additive nod bump for `micro_nod`; instantiate `BlinkScheduler` for non-rigged characters; verify composition with in-flight pose blends (as v2). **Upgrade:** replace uniform-jitter blink intervals with the empirical model — log-normal inter-blink intervals, blink-rate modulation by speaking state (speakers blink more at phrase boundaries, less mid-clause), and a forced blink within ~300 ms after each shot cut (cut-masking, a century-old editor's trick that makes cuts feel smoother for free). Blink events come from `ProsodyTrack` phrase boundaries.\n\n**B5. Camera: one spring** — retire `ease_out_cubic`, feed `smooth_char()` raw shot presets, tune `zeta ∈ [0.7, 1.0]` against the telemetry CSV (as v2). **Upgrade:** camera targets become a `CameraScript` (C-5), and add ±1–2 px slow drift on long static holds (turn (h)) — locked-off digital stillness is what makes puppets feel dead.\n\n**B6 (new). Secondary motion & follow-through.**\nThe rig gets a lightweight spring-hierarchy pass: hair/dupatta/collar sprites (whatever the character has) driven by damped springs keyed off torso/head velocity, evaluated after pose blending. This is the 12-principles \"follow-through\" that makes B1's blends read as *weight* instead of interpolation. Pure math + existing compositing; zero new dependencies. Acceptance: turn (a) at 50% speed — trailing elements settle 2–4 frames after the primary, never oscillate visibly at 100% speed.\n\n**B7 (new). Idle life: breathing + micro-sway + gaze saccades.**\n- Breathing: 2–4 px torso scale oscillation, ~12–16 cycles/min, amplitude modulated down while speaking.\n- Micro-sway: low-amplitude Perlin/OU-noise drift on torso lean — *seeded deterministically per turn* (determinism law).\n- Gaze: eye-pupil sprite offsets with saccade statistics (fixation 200–600 ms, instant jumps, small amplitude), biased toward \"camera\" with occasional glances toward the blackboard/diagram region.\nAcceptance: turn (h) — the 60-second derivation read — is watchable without the character feeling like a paused video. This item is cheap and transforms perceived production value more than any other single motion item after B1.\n\n**B8 (new). Prosody-driven head motion.**\nMap `ProsodyTrack` F0 and energy to subtle head pitch/roll: rises on pitch peaks, small forward emphasis nods on stressed syllables (energy peaks aligned to word boundaries from C-1). Rule-based, 3 tunable gains, layered additively *under* explicit gestures. This is what makes the character look like it *means* the sentence. Acceptance: turn (e) blind A/B (harness shuffles filenames): B8-on picked ≥4/5 times by you a week apart.\n\n**B9 (new). Gesture selection & smear system.**\n- Direction logic (Track F) emits `GestureScript` events from text cues (numbers → counting gesture, \"dekho\" → point, questions → open palm) + prosodic emphasis.\n- For fast pose transitions, add 1–2 authored **smear/multiple frames** (E-track asset type) triggered automatically when inter-pose displacement per frame exceeds a threshold — the classical 2D solution to exactly the ghosting problem, complementary to B1's blur and much cheaper than segmented limbs. Gate 1's review should explicitly compare smears-vs-segmentation before commissioning E3.\n\n---\n\n## Part VII — Track C: Speech (the crown of the plan)\n\n*(Reordering logic from v2 preserved: taxonomy → adapter → quality passes ∥ timing gate. C1–C5 upgraded; C6 rebuilt for the all-free constraint; C7–C8 new.)*\n\n**C1. Unify — and extend — the taxonomy.**\n`visemes.py`'s 6-class system becomes canonical for both render paths; retire the 4-class and 3-state mappings; retarget `MouthBlender` (as v2). **Upgrade the inventory itself to a 10-class Hindi-first set** (designed *with* E1 so art and code land together via the `SpriteManifest`):\n\n| Class | Covers | Why it earns a slot |\n|---|---|---|\n| REST | silence, pauses | baseline |\n| BILABIAL | प फ ब भ म, p/b/m | full closure — *dominant* in coarticulation |\n| LABIODENTAL | व f/v loanwords | lip-teeth contact, very visible |\n| DENTAL_ALVEOLAR | त थ द ध न स ज़ल, t/d/n/s/l | tongue-forward, slight opening |\n| RETROFLEX_VELAR | ट ठ ड ढ ण क ख ग घ | merged — tongue invisible; art shares shape with mid-open |\n| OPEN_A | आ अ (stressed) | wide open |\n| MID_E | ए ऐ, mid vowels | mid aperture, unrounded |\n| CLOSED_I | इ ई, y | spread lips |\n| ROUNDED_TENSE | ऊ उ, w | tight rounding |\n| ROUNDED_LAX | ओ औ | loose rounding |\n\nRetroflexes deliberately do *not* get a unique mouth shape (tongue posture is barely visible externally) — that's a documented ADR, not an omission. Unit tests over the fixture wordlist (conjuncts, virama, all matras, anusvara/chandrabindu, Latin loanwords — as v2) **plus** Hypothesis fuzzing per Phase 0, **plus** the Hinglish turn (i) and numbers turn (j), which force text normalization (`indic-nlp-library`, num2words-style expansion for units — all free) to exist *before* G2P rather than being discovered as a crash in month 6.\n\n**C2. Timing adapter v0** — exactly as v2. Uniform splits wrapped as `PhonemeEvent`s, zero visual change, pure plumbing. From here C3–C5, C7–C8, and Gate 2 are fully parallel.\n\n**C3. Real coarticulation** — as v2 (raised-cosine `VisemeTrack.sample()`, window ~40–60% of the shorter neighbor, both sprites composited, boundary-alpha unit test). **Change from v2:** per-class dominance is no longer optional. With the C1 inventory, BILABIAL and LABIODENTAL are *dominant* (must reach full closure/contact; vowels deform around them) from day one — the 10-class art makes half-closed lips on म look broken, so Cohen–Massaro-style dominance functions ship with v1 of the blend. Acceptance: boundary unit test; turn (c) no mouth pops; turn (d) plosives reach full closure (verified by sampling the composite alpha at bilabial midpoints — a *unit test*, not an eyeball).\n\n**C4. Amplitude done right** — as v2: attack ~15 ms / release ~100 ms envelope on RMS (20–30 ms window) before `mouth_openness()`; fed from a compressed pre-reverb preview tap (add the tap if 0.1 says none exists); `_Q_OPEN ≥ 1/16`. **Upgrades:** (i) the envelope reads from `ProsodyTrack` (C-7) so it's computed once; (ii) add a *noise-gate floor* tuned on whispered turn (k) so soft delivery doesn't render as mumbling; (iii) envelope parameters live in C-3 constants with the telemetry CSV plotting them per turn. Acceptance: no flutter on sustained vowels; closed within 250 ms of silence; full bilabial closures jointly with C3.\n\n**C5. Hindi schwa deletion — detect, don't just predict.**\nImplement Ohala's rule with v2's guards (word-final deletion; medial VC_CV deletion; no illegal clusters; respect explicit conjuncts; morpheme-boundary awareness where detectable) and the test set (fixture wordlist + ~30 hand-verified Sanskrit-heavy JEE physics/chemistry terms). **The definitive upgrade:** the ground truth for whether a schwa was pronounced *is the TTS audio itself*. Once Gate 2's aligner exists, add **dual-hypothesis alignment**: align both the schwa-present and schwa-deleted phoneme sequences against the actual audio; whichever scores better *is what the voice actually said*. The rule engine becomes the fast path; alignment-based detection becomes the oracle that (a) auto-grades the rule engine across the whole corpus, and (b) resolves the genuinely ambiguous Sanskrit-heavy terms empirically. Schwa handling stops being a linguistics bet and becomes a measurement.\n\n**C6. GATE 2 — timing source (rebuilt: the all-free ranking).**\nAzure is **out** (paid tier). The free landscape is genuinely better than the paid one here, because the definitive source isn't an aligner at all:\n\n1. **T1 — Native durations from a local VITS-family TTS (expected winner, and the endgame).**\n   VITS-architecture models contain a *duration predictor*: at synthesis time the model literally decides how many acoustic frames each input phoneme occupies. Extract those integer durations (a small patch to the inference code — the values already exist in memory as `w_ceil`) and you get **synthesizer-truth timing**: zero alignment error, zero extra inference, deterministic, offline, free. At 22.05 kHz / 256-sample hop that's ~11.6 ms resolution — sub-frame at 25 fps *by construction*.\n   Candidates to audition (in order): **AI4Bharat Indic Parler-TTS / Indic-TTS (IIT Madras collaboration)** — open weights, built for Indian languages, strongest Hindi naturalness in the open ecosystem; **Piper** (MIT, tiny, fast, has community `hi_IN` voices — audition quality); the maintained **coqui-ai TTS fork** (idiap) with a Hindi VITS. Evaluation: blind MOS sheet (harness-shuffled) against current edge-tts voices on 5 script excerpts + turn (i) Hinglish handling + license-ledger check. If naturalness is acceptable → this is the terminal answer and the pipeline becomes fully offline.\n2. **T2 — Bridge: keep `edge-tts` audio + free forced alignment.**\n   If no local voice beats `hi-IN-SwaraNeural` yet (honest possibility), keep edge-tts as the *bridge* (free but cloud, unofficial, could break — named risk) and align its output locally: **Montreal Forced Aligner** (MIT) with its published **Hindi (Common Voice) acoustic model + dictionary**, evaluated against hand-marked `manual`-source boundaries on 3 reference clips at ±40 ms word-level tolerance. MFA is the safest free Hindi aligner.\n   Secondary fallback: CTC-segmentation with an open Hindi wav2vec2 (e.g., ai4bharat indicwav2vec — *check checkpoint license*; several strong multilingual CTC checkpoints are CC-BY-NC and therefore ledger-banned if you monetize). WhisperX last (Hindi alignment quality is the known weak point — same ±40 ms validation before trusting it).\n3. **T3 — Uniform splits (adapter v0)** remain only if T1 and T2 both fail, which is very unlikely; C3–C5 still deliver most of their value on fake timing, which is exactly why C2 decoupled them.\n\nWhichever lane is live, the adapter contract means it's a drop-in, and **the harness SyncNet score is the referee**: acceptance is LSE-C/LSE-D materially better than adapter v0 on turns (c), (d), (g), plus bilabial closures within one frame of audible onsets on turn (c). Both the bridge and the endgame produce an ADR.\n\n**C7 (new). Prosody extraction.** Implement `ProsodyTrack` (C-4 contract): parselmouth/CREPE F0 + RMS energy + voicing, computed once post-synthesis, cached by audio hash. Consumers: C4 envelope, B7 breathing modulation, B8 head motion, B9 gesture emphasis, C8 pauses. One extraction, five features.\n\n**C8 (new). Pause, breath & phrase modeling.** Detect inter-phrase silences from `ProsodyTrack`; classify short (beat) vs long (breath). Long pauses get: mouth to REST via normal coarticulation, a subtle breath chest-rise (hooks B7), and optionally a quiet breath sample in the mix (free breath one-shots exist; or synthesize filtered noise). Rapid-fire turn (g) and derivation turn (h) are the test beds. This is the difference between \"lip-synced\" and \"a person talking.\"\n\n---\n\n## Part VIII — Track D: Render & Color Correctness\n\n*(v2's Track D, completed to the end of the road.)*\n\n- **One affine per sprite:** squash/stretch × lean × final scale composed into a single matrix, one resample, high-quality filter. Acceptance: VMAF/SSIM vs. baseline shows *global sharpening* (the rare fix where the whole diff should improve).\n- **Linear-light everything:** decode sRGB → linear **float32** (not just 16-bit int) for the full composite stack — not only bloom/halation/grade, but *alpha blending itself* (compositing in gamma space subtly darkens every anti-aliased sprite edge; in a cutout pipeline that's every edge on screen). Re-encode once at the end. Bloom threshold re-tuned (gamma-space thresholds are wrong in linear by construction — budgeted pass on turn (f), reviewed as a look change, not a regression).\n- **Optional, gated:** OCIO-managed config if the look pipeline grows LUTs; skip if plain sRGB↔linear suffices — an ADR either way.\n- **Encode:** explicit `-colorspace bt709 -color_primaries bt709 -color_trc bt709 -color_range tv`; `ffprobe` assertion in the harness; add `-movflags +faststart` and a pinned CRF/preset chosen once via VMAF-vs-filesize sweep (plotted, ADR'd, never touched again).\n- **Subtle film grain / dither** at final encode (free, one ffmpeg filter) — kills banding in flat blackboard-background regions that even 10-bit intermediates can leave, and adds perceived warmth.\n\n---\n\n## Part IX — Tracks E & F: Art and Direction\n\n**Track E — Art (all-free toolchain: Krita for raster sprites, Inkscape for vector, Blender for any cutout-rig experiments; all libre).**\n- **E1. Mouth set for the C1 ten-class inventory** — supersedes v2's \"8–10 Preston-Blair\": the inventory *is* the spec now, delivered against the `SpriteManifest` so integration is zero-code. Per-class notes for the artist (closure discipline for BILABIAL, teeth-on-lip for LABIODENTAL, tense vs lax rounding) travel with the manifest. Commission **day 1** — longest lead time, zero dependencies.\n- **E2. Emotion→expression gap** — sprites for `curious/enthusiastic/confident/explaining/dramatic`, *jointly* with Track F constraining the director's emotion vocabulary to a closed ontology (v2's insight: cheapest fix for an unmapped emotion is to stop emitting it; right fix is some of each). The ontology lives in one place (C-3 style) and a CI check asserts every emitter-legal emotion has a manifest entry.\n- **E3. Segmented limbs** — iff Gate 1 passes, *and* Gate 1 explicitly compares against B9 smears first (smears are dramatically cheaper and classical 2D answers fast-motion with them, not with more joints).\n- **E4 (new). Smear/breakdown frames** for the 4–6 highest-traffic gesture transitions (feeds B9).\n- **E5 (new). Style bible** — one reference sheet (palette, line weight, lighting direction, sprite resolution discipline) so every future commission matches. Written once, referenced forever.\n\n**Track F — Direction & Pedagogy (new track; v2 touched it via 0.1's scriptwriter question).**\n- **F1.** Closed **emotion ontology** + closed **gesture vocabulary** in `prompts.py` — the director may only emit what the pipeline can honor (validated at parse time, not render time).\n- **F2.** Director emits `GestureScript`/`CameraScript` (C-5) — beats, emphasis words, shot intents — making direction diffable and testable as text, decoupled from rendering.\n- **F3.** **Camera language rules** encoded once: cut on new concept, slow push-in during derivations, cut-masking blinks (B4), never two cuts <2 s apart except for deliberate emphasis. A lint over `CameraScript`, not a hope.\n- **F4.** Pedagogy pass (later, optional): emphasis auto-derived from script structure (formula introductions, \"note this\" markers) feeding B8/B9 — the point where direction quality compounds automatically with every video.\n\n---\n\n## Part X — Track G (optional research tier — the \"no limit\" shelf)\n\nOnly after Milestones 1–5, each gated on a demonstrated gap the classical stack can't close, each ADR'd, each license-checked. Listed so ambition has a home *outside* the critical path:\n- **G1. Learned audio-driven gesture** (open co-speech gesture models) replacing/augmenting rule-based `GestureScript` — gate: rule-based gestures feel repetitive across ≥10 full videos.\n- **G2. Neural inbetweening** (open video-interpolation models, e.g. RIFE-class — check license) to synthesize breakdown frames between poses — gate: B9 smears + B1 blur still insufficient AND Gate 1 failed on art cost.\n- **G3. Local voice upgrade/cloning** for a consistent brand voice fully offline — gate: T1 lane adopted and a better open voice model appears (the adapter makes swapping free).\n- **G4. Auto-QC:** SyncNet + VMAF + silence/black-frame detectors run on every *production* render, not just harness runs — a free, permanent quality floor once thresholds are calibrated.\n\n---\n\n## Part XI — Sequencing: Epochs, Gates, Dependency Graph\n\n```\nEpoch 0 (wks 1–3):    Phase −1 governance → 0.1 recon → 0.2 harness (baselines locked)\nEpoch 1 (wks 3–6):    Track A (A1–A4) ∥ B2 ∥ E1+E2 commissioned ∥ F1\n                      == Milestone 1: correct foundation, nothing judged stale ==\nEpoch 2 (wks 6–12):   B1 → GATE 1 (vs. B9 smears) → B3?/E3? ; B4 ; B5 ; C1 → C2\n                      == Milestone 2: motion c